 "_about": "Seconds each test file takes in one CI shard (setup + call + teardown, coverage on). scripts/ci_test_shards.py balances the shards on these; a file not listed weighs its test count times per_test_seconds_default. Measured 2026-10-12 per file from the shard reports of one CI run of PR #87, both Python versions (run 38310501963 on 29ee0835), each file's largest measurement, so a slower runner or Python version never under-weighs it; the refresh added the 16 test files written since the 2026-10-09 table, which had grown to 15% of a run's seconds, and split the two files over the 750 s per-file ceiling (tests/test_p792_reserved.py at 873.5 s, tests/test_factory_loop.py at 758.7 s) into themed files. Each part weighs its file's CI seconds times the part's largest share of the file's local seconds (setup + call + teardown, SQLite only, as on a shard) over every local session of the same test ids, so no one session's split under-weighs a part: tests/test_factory_loop.py's 758.7 s over 2 sessions (its parts sum to 767.4 s), and tests/test_p792_reserved.py's over 4 sessions from 1132.4 s, not 873.5 s, because the file grew from 124 to 161 test ids after 29ee0835: 1132.4 = (873.5 / 167.384) x 216.997, the CI seconds per local second of 29ee0835's file in one local session times the grown file's local seconds in the next (its parts sum to 1295.5 s). The next refresh replaces these estimates with the parts' own measurements. (The first table, 2026-09-27, spread each 72-test progress line's elapsed time evenly over its tests, which gave a slow file's seconds to its neighbours: P-740.) Refresh: download the test-shard-py* artifacts of one or more CI runs, both Python versions, and run `python3 scripts/ci_test_shards.py weights <every shard-*.json>`: it keeps each file's largest measurement, never a sum. The required test (py...) aggregators fail when the table goes stale.",
